{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792203274,"nonce":"Y19F15R1xjbHDlIegBnAvvIIMFM=","title":"Method calls on a structure typedef bind to stdlib runtime functions by name","message":"typedef MyIt\u003cT\u003e = { function hasNext():Bool; function next():T; }\n    class C { ... hasNext/next ... }\n    final it:MyIt\u003cInt\u003e = new C();\n    while (it.hasNext()) trace(it.next());   // crashes\n\nFor an Anonymous receiver, try_stdlib_runtime_dispatch treats the method as owner-less and scans every stdlib class for a same-named row, so hasNext/next become ArrayIterator_hasNext/next. That is right for an Iterator\u003cT\u003e holding an array iterator, and for structural views of arrays (Issue3071, Issue2889 depend on it), but wrong for any other value. Restricting the scan to iterator-shaped structures lost those rows; the fallback for a non-matching value needs a by-name dynamic call instead.\n\nThe coroutine work only declines the scan for structures with a coroutine member (AsyncIterator).","files":null}]}